ployee's identity. Two of the four options have been checked, and the page has a dark blue sidebar with menu options.\n\nStep 4: Temporary password issued\nNavigation: CoreID Admin Console → user profile → Reset Password\nSelect Generate Temporary Password. Leave Force change at next sign-in checked — this is the default and it must not be disabled.\n\n### Visual Inspection Report (Image 4 (inline, following the text above)) [Confidence: 0.95 / High]:\nThe image shows a screenshot of a password reset page for an employee, with a white background and a blue button that says \"Generate Temporary Password\". The page has a title that reads \"Reset Password - J. Employee\" and a temporary password field with a checkbox next to it.\n\n*   **Title**: \"Reset Password - J. Employee\"\n*   **Temporary Password Field**: A field to enter a temporary password, with a checkbox next to it that says \"Force change at next sign-in\"\n*   **Button**: A blue button that says \"Generate Temporary Password\"\n*   **Background**: White\n\n**Extracted Text:**\n\n*   None\n\n**Errors and Warnings:**\n\n*   None visible\n\n**Likely Issue Category and Severity:**\n\n*   Category: Account/authentication\n*   Severity: Medium\n\n**Observed Facts vs Inference:**\n\n*   **Observed Facts:**\n    *   The page is a password reset page for an employee.\n    *   The page has a temporary password field with a checkbox next to it.\n    *   There is a blue button that says \"Generate Temporary Password\".\n*   **Inference:**\n    *   The employee may have forgotten their password and is trying to reset it.\n    *   The temporary password will be used to force a change at the next sign-in.\n\n**Confidence Score and Notes:**\n\n*   CONFIDENCE SCORE: 0.95\n*   CONFIDENCE LEVEL: High\n*   CONFIDENCE REASON: The image is clear and the text is legible, making it easy to understand the context of the page.\n\nStep 5: Temporary password relayed securely\nNavigation: Phone / voice channel — not logged in CoreID\nRead the temporary password to the caller verbally over the verified phone line. Never send it by email, chat, or ticket comment in plain text. See [CISA's guidance on requiring strong passwords](https://www.cisa.gov/secure-our-world/require-strong-passwords) for the reasoning behind credential-handling policy.\nStep 6: Ticket resolved with audit note\nNavigation: Ticketing system → ticket → Resolution notes\nLog what was done (identity checks matched, temporary password issued, force-change enabled) and set the ticket to Resolved. This note is what satisfies the audit trail requirement — an admin-mediated reset with no note is a compliance gap.\n\n### Visual Inspection Report (Image 5 (inline, following the text above)) [Confidence: 0.90 / High]:\nThe image shows a screenshot of the CoreID Admin Console, with a ticket resolution page displayed. The page is titled \"Ticket #SOL-84213 - Resolution\" and has a status of \"Resolved\".\n\n*   **CoreID Admin Console**\n    *   The CoreID Admin Console is a web-based interface for managing user accounts and permissions.\n    *   It provides a centralized location for administrators to view and manage user information, including passwords, roles, and access levels.\n*   **Ticket #SOL-84213 - Resolution**\n    *   The ticket is titled \"Ticket #SOL-84213 - Resolution\" and has a status of \"Resolved\".\n    *   This indicates that the issue associated with this ticket has been resolved.\n*   **Status**\n    *   The status of the ticket is \"Resolved\", indicating that the issue has been fixed.\n    *   This suggests that the administrator has successfully resolved the problem and the user's account is now functioning correctly.\n*   **Resolution notes**\n    *   The resolution notes section provides additional information about the steps taken to resolve the issue.\n    *   It includes details such as the identity verified (2 of 4 checks), temporary password issued via CoreID, force-change-at-next-sign-in enabled, and no