      "content": "You are an Expert Human SOP Faithfulness Inspector for an IT Automation System.\n\nYour task is to perform a rigorous Chain-of-Thought (CoT) inspection completely comparing a Raw Human SOP document text against a Converted OKF Steps JSON.\n\n## RAW HUMAN SOP TEXT:\nReset a Locked-Out User's Password via CoreID Admin Console\nL1 Escalation Runbook — Solandra Corp IT Support\nsop_id\nSOL-PWD-RESET-L1-002\nresolution_type\nescalate_only\naudience\nL1 Support Agent (authorized admin)\nrequired_role\nCoreID: Password Reset Admin\norg\nSolandra Corp\napplication\nCoreID Admin Console (mock)\nPurpose\nWhen a user's self-service password reset fails or the user has no working verification channel, the ticket escalates to L1. An authorized L1 agent with the Password Reset Admin role in CoreID resets the password directly from the admin console.\nBecause the agent's own CoreID session is already MFA-authenticated and role-gated, this flow does not send a one-time verification code to the user. That code-based check exists to authenticate an otherwise-unverified self-service claimant; an admin acting under an authenticated, role-scoped session establishes identity through the caller checklist instead (Step 3).\nProcedure\nInputs\nOpen ticket with the requester's employee ID and manager name on file\nYour own authenticated CoreID session with the Password Reset Admin role assigned\nCaller identity verification checklist (minimum 2 of 4 items matched)\nSteps\nStep 1: Ticket reviewed\nNavigation: Ticketing system → open ticket → Requester Details panel\nConfirm the ticket category reads Password Reset — Escalated, and note the requester's employee ID and manager name. ⚠️ Do not proceed if the ticket is missing a manager name — request it from the requester first.\n\nStep 2: User located and lock confirmed\nNavigation: CoreID Admin Console → Users → Search bar\nSearch by employee ID. Confirm the account status badge shows Locked before continuing — do not reset a password on an account that isn't actually locked without further checks.\n\nStep 3: Caller identity verified\nNavigation: CoreID Admin Console → user profile → Identity Verification panel\nAsk the caller for at least 2 of: employee ID, manager's name, department, or last 4 digits of the phone on file. ✅ Do not send a one-time code to the user — your own admin session already satisfies the authentication requirement; this checklist is what verifies the caller is who they claim to be. If fewer than 2 items match, stop and escalate to your team lead rather than proceeding.\n\nStep 4: Temporary password issued\nNavigation: CoreID Admin Console → user profile → Reset Password\nSelect Generate Temporary Password. Leave Force change at next sign-in checked — this is the default and it must not be disabled.\n\nStep 5: Temporary password relayed securely\nNavigation: Phone / voice channel — not logged in CoreID\nRead the temporary password to the caller verbally over the verified phone line. Never send it by email, chat, or ticket comment in plain text. See [CISA's guidance on requiring strong passwords](https://www.cisa.gov/secure-our-world/require-strong-passwords) for the reasoning behind credential-handling policy.\nStep 6: Ticket resolved with audit note\nNavigation: Ticketing system → ticket → Resolution notes\nLog what was done (identity checks matched, temporary password issued, force-change enabled) and set the ticket to Resolved. This note is what satisfies the audit trail requirement — an admin-mediated reset with no note is a compliance gap.\n\nOutputs\nUser account unlocked with a valid temporary password\nForce-change-at-next-sign-in flag enabled\nTicket resolved with an audit note logged\nFAQs\nQ: What if the caller can't confirm at least 2 of the 4 identity items?\nDo not issue a reset. Escalate to your team lead, or ask the requester to retry self-service once they regain access to a verified phone or email.\nQ: Why doesn't this flow send a verification code the way self-service does?\nThe self-service code exists to authenticate an anonymous claimant with no other proof of identity. Here, identity is established two ways at once: your own CoreID session is already MFA-authenticated and scoped to the Password Reset Admin role, and the caller checklist independently confirms the requester is who they claim to be. Adding a code on top of that verifies nothing new — it would just be an unnecessary step to a channel the caller may not have working anyway, which is usually why the ticket was escalated in the first place.\n\n## CONVERTED OKF STEPS JSON:\n[\n  {\n    \"step_number\": 1,\n    \"sop_step_description\": \"Confirm the ticket category and extract the requester's employee ID and manager name.\",\n    \"step_type\": \"tool_call\",\n    \"step_reasoning\": \"Gate 1-4 passed: The raw SOP explicitly describes confirming ticket details, which aligns with the 'search_user' function in the Solandra platform. This function retrieves user details, including employee_id and manager_name, which are required for subsequent steps. The 'employee_id' is mapped to {{{{entities.employee_id}}}}.\",\n    \"platform\": \"solandra\",\n    \"function_name\": \"search_user\",\n    \"comm_inputs\": {\n      \"employee_id\": \"{{{{entities.employee_id}}}}\"\n    },\n    \"command\": null,\n    \"fallback_command\": null,\n    \"fallback_sop_id\": null,\n    \"parse_instruction\": null,\n    \"execute_if\": null,\n    \"timeout_seconds\": 30\n  },\n  {\n    \"step_number\": 2,\n    \"sop_step_description\": \"Verify the caller's identity using the CoreID Admin Console identity verification checklist.\",\n    \"step_type\": \"tool_call\",\n    \"step_reasoning\": \"Gate 1-4 passed: The raw SOP describes verifying the caller's identity using at least 2 of 4 identity items. This matches the 'verify_caller_identity' function in the Solandra platform. The required inputs (employee_id, manager_name, department, phone_last4) are mapped to entities or prior step outputs. The 'user_id' is derived from the prior step's output.\",\n    \"platform\": \"solandra\",\n    \"function_name\": \"verify_caller_identity\",\n    \"comm_inputs\": {\n      \"user_id\": \"{{{{variables.user_id}}}}\",\n      \"employee_id\": \"{{{{entities.employee_id}}}}\",\n      \"manager_name\": \"{{{{entities.manager_name}}}}\",\n      \"department\": \"{{{{entities.department}}}}\",\n      \"phone_last4\": \"{{{{entities.phone_last4}}}}\"\n    },\n    \"command\": null,\n    \"fallback_command\": null,\n    \"fallback_sop_id\": null,\n    \"parse_instruction\": null,\n    \"execute_if\": \"variables.account_locked == 'true'\",\n    \"timeout_seconds\": 30\n  },\n  {\n    \"step_number\": 3,\n    \"sop_step_description\": \"Generate a temporary password for the locked user and enable force change at next sign-in.\",\n    \"step_type\": \"tool_call\",\n    \"step_reasoning\": \"Gate 1-4 passed: The raw SOP describes generating a temporary password for the locked user, which matches the 'generate_temporary_password' function in the Solandra platform. The 'user_id' is derived from the prior step's output. The function ensures 'force_change_at_next_sign_in' is enabled by default.\",\n    \"platform\": \"solandra\",\n    \"function_name\": \"generate_temporary_password\",\n    \"comm_inputs\": {\n      \"user_id\": \"{{{{variables.user_id}}}}\"\n    },\n    \"command\": null,\n    \"fallback_command\": null,\n    \"fallback_sop_id\": null,\n    \"parse_instruction\": null,\n    \"execute_if\": \"variables.identity_verified == 'true'\",\n    \"timeout_seconds\": 90\n  },\n  {\n    \"step_number\": 4,\n    \"sop_step_description\": \"Resolve the ticket with an audit note detailing the actions taken.\",\n    \"step_type\": \"tool_call\",\n    \"step_reasoning\": \"Gate 1-4 passed: The raw SOP describes resolving the ticket with an audit note, which matches the 'resolve_ticket' function in the Solandra platform. The 'ticket_id' is mapped to {{{